# lattice

your knowledge, on your ship.

Pages in markdown, gemtext, or HTML — or Hoon pages that compute their own content. A private, tagged memory you share with your AI. Full-text search across all of it, and peer-to-peer publishing: every published page is addressed as urb://~ship/path and travels ship-to-ship over remote scry. No DNS, no web server, no host in the middle.

(You may well be reading this page through lattice right now.)

## One store, every surface

* The ship — a lattice nexus in the grubbery framework. Pages and knowledge live as grubs in the vault; published pages answer remote scry; followed files push you updates.
* The web — a reader, a workspace editor with live preview and drag-and-drop upload, a knowledge browser, and an installable PWA — served straight from the ship. lattice hosts its own client; there is nothing to install.
* The filesystem — lattice-fs mounts your pages as local files. grep runs from RAM; edit in your own editor.
* The agents — MCP knowledge tools compiled into the ship, served at /grubbery/mcp. Your assistant's memory lives on your ship.

## What pages look like

Publish a page, and anyone on the network can read it — instantly, straight from your ship:

```
# ~nisfeb's notebook

Welcome. Everything here is a page on my ship.

=> urb://~nisfeb/notes/urbit  Notes on Urbit
=> urb://~nisfeb/now          What I'm doing now
```

A page can also be Hoon that computes its content — state, commands, dependencies on other pages — or go clearweb-public, served over plain HTTP from your ship.

## What it isn't

* Not a host. Bring your own ship — yours, a friend's, or a hosted one.
* Not the HTTP web. Published pages are Urbit-native and move between ships over remote scry.
* Not on the app stores. Sideload the APK and desktop installers from GitHub Releases.

## Privacy

The only server lattice talks to is your ship. No analytics, no third-party SDKs, no telemetry. Published pages are public by design; the knowledge store is private by default — foreign access is deny-by-default at the ship.

